      "text": "To find the minimum value of the expression \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\), we can use the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM Inequality). The AM-GM Inequality states that for non-negative real numbers, the arithmetic mean is at least the geometric mean. Specifically, for three non-negative real numbers \\(x, y, z\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x + y + z}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{xyz}.\n\\]\n\nWe will apply this inequality to each term in the sum \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\). To do this, we need to combine these terms into a single expression that fits into the AM-GM inequality. Notice that we can rewrite the sum as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} = \\left(\\frac{a}{2b}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{b}{4c}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{c}{8a}\\right).\n\\]\n\nTo use AM-GM, we need to find a common factor that will allow us to group the terms in a way that each term is positive and can be paired. Let's multiply each term by a suitable factor to achieve this. We will multiply by 6 (the least common multiple of 2, 4, and 8) to make the terms clear:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6a}{4b} + \\frac{6b}{12c} + \\frac{6c}{24a} = \\frac{3a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we can apply the AM-GM inequality to the three terms \\(\\frac{3a}{2b}\\), \\(\\frac{b}{4c}\\), and \\(\\frac{c}{8a}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{3a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}}{3} \\geq \\sqrt[3]{\\left(\\frac{3a}{2b}\\right) \\left(\\frac{b}{4c}\\right) \\left(\\frac{c}{8a}\\right)}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{\\left(\\frac{3a}{2b}\\right) \\left(\\frac{b}{4c}\\right) \\left(\\frac{c}{8a}\\right)} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3a \\cdot b \\cdot c}{2b \\cdot 4c \\cdot 8a}} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{3bc}{64abc}} = \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{64}} = \\frac{1}{4}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\frac{3a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}}{3} \\geq \\frac{1}{4},\n\\]\n\nwhich implies\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} \\geq 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}.\n\\]\n\nTo check if this minimum value can be achieved, we need to see if there exist positive real numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) such that equality holds in the AM-GM inequality. Equality holds if and only if\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} = \\frac{b}{4c} = \\frac{c}{8a}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(k = \\frac{a}{2b} = \\frac{b}{4c} = \\frac{c}{8a}\\). Then we have the system of equations:\n\n1. \\(a = 2b\\),\n2. \\(b = 4c\\),\n3. \\(c = 8a\\).\n\nFrom the first equation, we have \\(a = 2b\\). Substituting \\(a = 2b\\) into the third equation, we get \\(c = 8(2b) = 16b\\). Substituting \\(c = 16b\\) into the second equation, we get \\(b = 4(16b)\\), which simplifies to \\(b = 64b\\). This is not possible unless \\(b = 0\\), but since \\(b\\) is a positive real number, we can set \\(b = 1\\) (arbitrary positive value). Then \\(a = 2\\) and \\(c = 16\\). Substituting these values back, we see that they satisfy all the equations, confirming that equality holds.\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}\\).",
