      "text": "The least possible value is \\(\\frac{3}{4}.\\) To determine the minimum possible value of the expression \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\), we can use the method of variable substitution and the AM-GM inequality.\n\nFirst, let's introduce new variables to simplify the expression:\n\\[ x = \\frac{a}{2b}, \\quad y = \\frac{b}{4c}, \\quad z = \\frac{c}{8a}. \\]\n\nWe need to find the minimum value of:\n\\[ x + y + z, \\]\nwhere \\( x, y, z > 0 \\).\n\nBy the AM-GM inequality, we have:\n\\[ x + y + z \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{xyz}. \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( xyz \\). Notice that:\n\\[ x = \\frac{a}{2b}, \\quad y = \\frac{b}{4c}, \\quad z = \\frac{c}{8a}. \\]\n\nMultiplying these together, we get:\n\\[ xyz = \\left( \\frac{a}{2b} \\right) \\left( \\frac{b}{4c} \\right) \\left( \\frac{c}{8a} \\right) = \\frac{a \\cdot b \\cdot c}{2b \\cdot 4c \\cdot 8a} = \\frac{abc}{64abc} = \\frac{1}{64}. \\]\n\nThus,\n\\[ x + y + z \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{64}} = 3\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{4^3}} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}. \\]\n\nTo check if this minimum value is achievable, consider the case when \\( x = y = z \\). Let \\( x = y = z = t \\). Then:\n\\[ x + y + z = 3t. \\]\n\nUsing \\( xyz = \\frac{1}{64} \\), we have:\n\\[ t^3 = \\frac{1}{64} \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{1}{4}. \\]\n\nSo, when \\( x = y = z = \\frac{1}{4} \\), we get:\n\\[ x + y + z = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}. \\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of the sum \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\) is indeed \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}\\).",
